# check-verbatim.allow -- matches that are known, deliberate and defended.
#
# TWO FORMS, and the difference is the whole point:
#
#     path/prefix/         a reason        every match under the path.  Blunt.  Only where a
#                                          whole file is interface by construction.
#     path <span-id>       a reason        ONE span in ONE file.  The normal form.
#
# The span id is printed beside every unexplained match; paste it here with a reason.  It is a
# HASH of the matched text and not the text itself, because writing the quotation into this file
# would put the borrowed words into the repository in order to record that we decided not to.
# It also changes when the span changes, so an edited quotation comes back for a fresh decision
# instead of quietly inheriting this one.
#
# Entries here are still REPORTED, so nobody can forget they exist; they just do not fail the
# check.  Adding one is a decision to be able to defend, not a way to make output quieter.  The
# question to answer in the reason is always the same: **is this text dictated, or was it
# chosen?**  If an author could have written it differently and still been correct, it does not
# belong here -- it belongs rewritten as a description.

include-modelb/	The Model-B header override: <sys/param.h> and <sys/immu.h> with the page shift
	changed from 11 to 12.  The macros that match -- ptob/btop/btopr, PAGEMASK, PAGEOFFSET,
	phystopfn, pfntophys, kvtopfn and the mmu_* forms -- are the kernel's compile-time ABI.
	Code compiled against them must agree with the kernel bit for bit, so their form is
	dictated by the interface: there is no other way to write "shift by the page shift" that
	still interoperates.  What this port changed is the constant; what it kept is the only
	expression that works.  Twelve lines, all one-liners, all interface.
	OPEN OPTION, recorded so the decision is not silently permanent: these two files could be
	GENERATED at build time from the reader's own installed headers, the way this project
	already ships the AmigaOS loader as patches rather than as files (amix-unix-boot).
	src/mk_modelb_sysroot.sh already builds the mirror sysroot and already has a geometry
	probe that would validate the result, so the instrument for the change exists.


# ============================================================================================
# SPAN IDS RECOMPUTED 2026-09-14.  The id is now the first 12 hex digits of the SHA-1 of the
# span's normalised token stream; it was SHA-256 before.  No decision below changed -- the same
# spans, the same paths, the same reasons.  Only the key is different.
#
# WHY.  The companion scanner on the other machine computes the same kind of id with SHA-1, so
# the two tools named one quotation with two different strings and neither could read the
# other's allowlist.  Truncated to 12 hex digits the two hashes are equally strong against the
# only collision that can occur here -- an accidental one, at a birthday bound four orders of
# magnitude above the number of spans this repository has -- so the algorithm was the cheap
# thing to concede and comparability the expensive thing to gain.  `;` entered COMMENT_LEAD in
# the same change and for the same reason; see span_id() and COMMENT_LEAD in check-verbatim.py.
#
# HOW THE 25 ENTRIES BELOW WERE MAPPED, and the one thing that went wrong, because it is the
# kind of thing that goes wrong again: this file was emptied, both versions of the checker were
# run over the same tree, and the reported spans were joined on (pass, label, line).  The first
# attempt joined on label and line ALONE and silently lost five entries -- a location can carry
# one span in the exact-line pass and a different, longer one in the joined-stream pass, and a
# commit-message label contains spaces, which a path-shaped pattern does not match.  The gate
# then read 5 unexplained, 42 known instead of 0 and 47, which is how it was caught.  The proof
# that the mapping is right is that this file explains every span again.
# ============================================================================================

# ============================================================================================
# SPAN DECISIONS, 2026-08-30.  The first joined-stream run of this gate produced 38 unexplained
# spans; eleven were closed by widening the boilerplate rules and by rewriting five quotations
# as descriptions (commit 258726f).  What follows is the residue, decided one span at a time.
# ============================================================================================

# -- the expression a byte patch converts -----------------------------------------------------
# These are not quotations for their own sake.  A byte patcher's entire claim is "this expression,
# at this address, becomes that one", and the expression is what makes the claim checkable against
# the disassembly.  Removing it would leave an address and an assertion with nothing to test.
# Note the framing this leaves open, and which the next pass over these files should take: the
# expression is derivable from the binary WE are patching, so it can be stated as what the code
# does rather than as what the source says.  Recorded here rather than done now.

src/patch_mincore.py 0a23a11fbe80	The mincore alignment gate: the source contract this patcher
	converts, quoted so the byte assertion at 0x585e2 has something to be checked against.
src/patch_ufsbmap.py 0e70e42544e0	The blocks-per-page expression ufs_bmap computes; the patch
	changes the page term of it and the canaries assert the neighbouring block terms.
src/segvn_softunlock_dbg.s 386db819e537	The anon-slot test this instrument sits in front of; the
	debug unit exists to report when it is reached and with what.
src/swapconf_dbg.s 40150dc199be	The swap-device lookup call whose arguments this instrument
	prints; the argument order IS what the probe reports.
test-tools/bmaptest.c 7f0dee654d3b	The allocation-size condition this test exercises, in the
	comment that states what the test is for.
test-tools/pgcold.c cfc7ae25aea5	The page-boundary condition under test, in the comment that
	states the contract the program checks.

# -- signatures and declarations: the identifiers ARE the interface ----------------------------
# A description of a function signature loses the thing that makes it usable -- the names and
# their order are the contract, and two implementations that agree on behaviour but not on the
# signature do not interoperate.  Same for a struct whose field order is an ABI.

docs/contracts/HAT-EXEC-AUDIT.md de46a631a063	as_exec's signature.  The audit is about argument
	handling, so the parameter names and their order are its subject rather than its decoration.
src/segkmem040.s 06f3366a081b	segkmem_setprot's signature, stated where this override has to
	match it exactly.
docs/archive/RAM-BEYOND-16MB-CODEX-TASK.md 110da59cbfcb	The bootinfo struct's first member.  The
	task is about reading this structure from a running kernel, so field order is the subject.
docs/archive/RAM-BEYOND-16MB-CODEX-TASK.md 5e152f20934a	The same struct's second member, for the
	same reason.
docs/archive/ISSUE40-AVAILRMEM-TASK.md 458300cf9cef	The USIZE declaration and its unit comment.
	The whole issue is that the constant's UNIT is 2048 bytes; the comment is the evidence.
src/fpe-compat/sys/cdefs.h 188280d0e371	BSD short type names, absent from AMIX.  There is one
	spelling of `typedef unsigned char u_char;` and code compiled against it must agree.
src/fpe-compat/sys/cdefs.h 04ed449b53ef	The same four typedefs, matched as a run.

# -- strings that identify a message or locate code in a binary --------------------------------
# A printf format string is how a line observed on a serial port is traced back to the code that
# printed it.  Describing it instead would make the identification unrepeatable.

docs/060-F4-M2-ATT5-RESULTS-260825.md 750a88356bb1	The swap-configuration diagnostic, quoted to
	identify which message the machine printed.
docs/060-F4-M2-ATT6-PREREG-260825.md 750a88356bb1	The same message, in the pre-registration that
	predicted it.
docs/A3091-WEDGE-PRESTUDY-260826.md 2655758084e6	The A3091 driver's own diagnostic call.  The
	format string is what appeared on the wire and the three argument names are the mapping this
	section exists to state; the section decodes exactly this line.

# -- output the machine printed, captured verbatim on purpose ----------------------------------
# These are transcripts.  The AT&T and Lachman copyright banner is printed by the kernel under
# test at every boot; a log that edited it would no longer be a record of what happened.

test-tools/issue10-amixadm-emu-repro-260729.log df03ad9b5de0	Kernel boot banner in a captured log.
test-tools/issue22-serial36-260728.log df03ad9b5de0	Kernel boot banner in a captured log.
test-tools/issue22-serial45-260728.log df03ad9b5de0	Kernel boot banner in a captured log.
test-tools/pagecreate-issue27-COLD-PROOF-260725.txt 5027022ff7cb	The contract expression quoted in
	a captured verification run; the file is a record of that run and is not edited after the fact.

# -- commit messages: recorded because they CANNOT be rewritten --------------------------------
# Five spans in four messages, found the first time this gate looked at messages at all.  They
# are contract expressions and one sentence from a source comment, all attributed where they
# appear.  A pushed commit message cannot be changed without rewriting published history, and
# this repository cites 83 commit hashes as provenance across 152 documents -- so the cost of
# removing five short quotations is every one of those references.  Decision, 2026-08-30: they
# stay, and they are recorded here so that the decision is visible rather than implied.
# The rule this pays for: the gate now sees a message BEFORE it is pushed, which is the only
# moment at which this is cheap.

commit 13544a9 message e2afe1e6d143	The zero-fill expression the patch converts, in the message
	that converted it.
commit 13544a9 message 73d98ee41241	One sentence from the source comment that states why the
	tail is zeroed.  The file copy of this quotation was rewritten as a description in 258726f;
	the message cannot be.
commit 9c6a886 message 5027022ff7cb	The page-boundary condition, in the message that converted it.
commit eab3c45 message 7f0dee654d3b	The allocation-size condition, in the message that converted it.
commit f35ddef message f573cd5e53ac	The device-mmap failure contract, in the message that
	converted it.
